---
import ToolPage from "../components/ToolPage.astro";
import Decay from "../components/calc/Decay.tsx";
import { u } from "../lib/site";
import Faq from "../components/Faq.astro";
import { EX } from "../data/worked-examples";
const D = EX.decay;
---
<ToolPage slug="decay">
  <Decay slot="tool" client:only="react" />
  <Fragment slot="prose">
    <h2>The one equation</h2>
    <p>
      Radioactive decay is a first-order process: the number of transformations per second is
      proportional to the number of atoms present, and nothing else. Temperature, pressure, chemical
      form and dilution do not change it. That gives A(t) = A₀·e<sup>−λt</sup>, where λ is the decay
      constant, ln 2 divided by the half-life. Every mode of this calculator is that equation solved
      for a different unknown.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because the exponent depends only on the ratio of elapsed time to half-life, it is often easier
      to think in half-lives than in years. One half-life leaves 50%, two leave 25%, seven leave
      under 1%, and ten leave about one part in a thousand. The rule of ten half-lives is why waste
      storage periods are quoted the way they are. For dosing, the note on
      {" "}<a href={u("/radiopharmaceutical-decay/")} class="underline">decay correction for
      radiopharmaceuticals</a> gives Tc-99m and F-18 at 15-minute steps and Lu-177 at 6-hour steps.
    </p>

    <h2>Finding a half-life from two measurements</h2>
    <p>
      Two activity measurements separated by a known interval determine a half-life exactly:
      T½ = ln 2 · Δt / ln(A₀/A₁). The arithmetic is trivial and the interpretation is not. The
      calculation cannot distinguish decay from anything else that removed activity between the two
      readings — a source leaking, a tracer adsorbing onto the container wall, a detector drifting,
      or a geometry that changed when the sample was repositioned. All of those produce an apparent
      half-life shorter than the true one.
    </p>
    <p>
      For that reason the result here is shown against the published half-life for the nuclide you
      picked. A difference of a few percent over a short interval usually means measurement
      uncertainty. A difference of tens of percent usually means something other than decay is
      happening, and the useful response is to look for it rather than to report a new half-life.
    </p>

    <h3>Choosing the interval</h3>
    <p>
      An interval much shorter than the half-life gives a ratio close to 1, and the logarithm of a
      number close to 1 amplifies counting uncertainty enormously. An interval much longer than the
      half-life drives the second measurement into background. The best precision comes from an
      interval of roughly one to three half-lives, where the ratio is between about 2 and 8.
    </p>

    <h2>Decay chains and ingrowth</h2>
    <p>
      When a daughter is itself radioactive, its activity starts at zero, rises as the parent feeds
      it, peaks, and then follows the parent down. The Bateman equations describe this, and the shape
      depends on which half-life is longer. When the parent is much longer-lived, the daughter
      reaches secular equilibrium and the two activities become equal — this is why a sealed Cs-137
      source is really emitting the 662 keV photon of its daughter Ba-137m, and why a Sr-90 source
      is a Y-90 beta source in practice.
    </p>
    <p>
      This calculator follows one nuclide; it does not build chains. The Bateman solution is applied
      along a single branch in the note on
      {" "}<a href={u("/natural-series/")} class="underline">natural decay series</a>. Real chains in
      the uranium and thorium series branch, and a branching ratio has to be carried through each step.
    </p>

    <h2>What the answer assumes</h2>
    <p>
      A single nuclide, no ingrowth from a parent, no losses
      other than decay, and a half-life taken from the evaluated nuclear data. Half-life values
      themselves carry uncertainty, and for a few nuclides different evaluations differ by enough to
      matter — Sr-90 is quoted between about 28.8 and 28.9 years depending on the compilation, which
      shifts a thirty-year projection by roughly half a percent.
    </p>

    <h2>Worked examples</h2>
    <h3>A technetium-99m dose drawn later in the morning</h3>
    <p>
      A vial is calibrated at 800&nbsp;MBq of Tc-99m at 07:00. The first patient is booked for
      10:30 and another for 13:00. How much activity is left in the vial at each time?
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Time</th><th class="text-right">Elapsed</th><th class="text-right">Activity</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>07:00 (calibration)</td><td class="num text-right">0&nbsp;h</td><td class="num text-right">800&nbsp;MBq</td></tr>
          <tr><td>10:30</td><td class="num text-right">3.5&nbsp;h</td><td class="num text-right">{D.tc1030}&nbsp;MBq</td></tr>
          <tr><td>13:00</td><td class="num text-right">6&nbsp;h</td><td class="num text-right">{D.tc1300}&nbsp;MBq</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Six hours is almost exactly one half-life of Tc-99m, so the vial holds half its calibrated
      activity by early afternoon. The same calculation run backwards gives the activity to order:
      a dose needed at 13:00 must be drawn as about twice that amount at 07:00. The vial is still
      measured in the dose calibrator before injection; the calculation tells you what to expect.
      Write the clock time of each draw on the syringe label as well as the activity. Without it the
      activity at injection cannot be reconstructed afterwards, which is the first thing asked when a
      scan looks wrong.
    </p>

    <h3>Identifying a vial from two measurements</h3>
    <p>
      An unlabelled vial is found in a hot lab. In the same counting position its net count rate is
      1520&nbsp;s⁻¹, and 24&nbsp;hours later 1181&nbsp;s⁻¹. Enter both rates and the interval in
      the “Find the half-life” mode.
    </p>
    <div class="not-prose my-3 pin-first table-fit" tabindex="0">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Result</th><th class="text-right">Value</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Half-life</td><td class="num text-right">{D.hlH}&nbsp;h ({D.hlD}&nbsp;d)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Mo-99, evaluated</td><td class="num text-right">{D.mo99D}&nbsp;d</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Au-198, evaluated</td><td class="num text-right">{D.au198D}&nbsp;d</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The result matches Mo-99, but Au-198 is close by. Over one day the rate only falls by about a
      fifth, so a one-percent error in either count rate moves the half-life by about
      {D.hlSensPct}%, which is wider than the gap between the two candidates. A gamma spectrum
      settles the question: Mo-99 shows its 740&nbsp;keV line and the 140&nbsp;keV line of its
      Tc-99m daughter, Au-198 a single line at 412&nbsp;keV. Counting the vial again a few days
      later helps as well: the longer the interval, the less a counting error moves the half-life, as
      the section on choosing the interval explains.
    </p>

    <Faq slug="decay" />
  </Fragment>
</ToolPage>
